// ======================================================================
// ROUND C-049 -- THE FRAME OF ITS OWN, AND WHY IT SAYS NO HERE
// ======================================================================
//
// Under Windows Certus takes the non-client area itself
// (WM_NCCALCSIZE) and draws the tabs into the title bar. Under
// X11 the situation is different: the bar belongs to the
// WINDOW MANAGER, not to the window. A program can switch it off with
// `_MOTIF_WM_HINTS`, but then what the
// user expects from his manager is gone too -- the keyboard shortcuts for
// moving, the tiling behaviour, the border for drag-resizing. And
// every manager does it differently.
//
// That is why this back end honestly says NO. `chrome.fi` asks before
// drawing (`window.frame_can`) and leaves out the window buttons under
// X11 -- there the manager draws them, as before. The bar
// thereby loses NOTHING of its ability; under X11 it simply has nothing to
// do.
//
// (Whoever wants it anyway can rebuild it with `_MOTIF_WM_HINTS` and
// `_NET_WM_MOVERESIZE`. That then belongs in a round of its own WITH
// a measuring rig on which more than one manager runs --
// not as an unproven side effect here.)
#[no_gc]
fn r_frame_can(x: *mut Platte) -> bool { return false }

